require "std_permutation.pil"
require "std_range_check.pil"
require "opids.pil"
require "mem/pil/mem.pil"
require "mem/pil/rom_data.pil"

/*
    Compact Memory (CompactMem)
    ===========================

    This machine is the fusion of THREE memory airs into a single one:

      · the mutable RAM area          -- `Mem`      (state-machines/mem/pil/mem.pil)
      · the free-input area           -- `InputData` (`Mem` with free_input_mem: 1)
      · the read-only program data    -- `RomData`  (state-machines/mem/pil/rom_data.pil)

    Unlike the three airs it fuses, this is NOT a machine that proves one area or another: EVERY
    row carries the three of them at once. Each area keeps its own lanes, its own air values and
    its own continuation chain; the blocks share nothing but the row index, and no constraint
    crosses from one block to another. Read this file as three independent airs stacked
    side by side.

        ROW i   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
                │ mem_*    : mem_lanes_x_row   lanes of the mutable RAM sequence   │
                │ input_*  : input_lanes_x_row lanes of the free-input sequence    │
                │ rom_*    : rom_lanes_x_row   lanes of the rom-data sequence      │
                └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

    Why
    ───

    The three areas are always proved together and each one always opens with a segment that is
    far from full -- `RomData` and `InputData` need exactly one instance per execution, and the
    first `Mem` segment comes with them. Three instances are therefore paid for what fits, row
    budget aside, in one. What costs is not the basic proof but the recursion over the number of
    instances, so merging the FIRST segment of each area into a single instance removes two
    instances from every execution.

    Every block here is sized exactly like the air it replaces (same rows, same lanes per row), so
    the first segment planned for `Mem`, `InputData` and `RomData` fits this air unchanged: the
    planners keep segmenting exactly as they do today, and only the assembly of the plans changes.
    The remaining segments (segment 1 onwards) are proved by the standalone airs.

    Is this really the same air, three times?
    ─────────────────────────────────────────

    It is meant to be, and it can be checked. Compile a PIL that instantiates `Mem`, `InputData`,
    `RomData` and `CompactMem` side by side with `-O output-constraints -O raw-constraints-format`,
    strip the block prefixes, and compare the constraint sets. Every constraint of the `Mem` and
    `RomData` airs appears verbatim in the `mem_` and `rom_` blocks, and the range checks match one
    for one. The only difference is in the free-input block, on purpose:

      · `mem.pil` emits `(1 - addr_changes) * (addr - previous_addr) === 0` TWICE on the immutable
        path -- once inside the immutable branch and once in the loop that follows the if/else --
        so `InputData` carries `lanes_x_row` redundant copies of it. The `input_` block emits it
        once. Same constraint, one copy instead of two.

    The bus interactions differ in shape but not in content: the three blocks share one gsum
    accumulator, so the standard library packs their terms into different clusters than it does
    when each air accumulates on its own.

    Continuations
    ─────────────

    Each block takes over segment 0 of its area's chain, and the standalone air continues from
    segment 1. The chains are the same ones (`MEMORY_CONTINUATION_ID` keyed by `base_address` for
    the two memory blocks, `ROM_DATA_CONTINUATION_ID` for the rom-data block), so an area's cycle
    is opened once, walked across the two airs and closed once.

    The global update that OPENS a cycle is emitted statically, once per air and regardless of how
    many instances the air gets, so it must NOT be emitted here: the standalone airs already do it.
    Both `mem.pil` and `rom_data.pil` guard it with a `proof` container that records which air
    opened which cycle, and the blocks below consult those same containers -- they find the cycle
    already opened by `Mem`, `InputData` and `RomData` and stay quiet. This is why `CompactMem` has
    to be instantiated AFTER them in `pil/zisk.pil`: whichever air comes first is the one that
    seeds, and the seed has to be gated by that area's `enable_flag`, which the standalone air is
    the one that knows.
*/

airtemplate CompactMem(const int N = 2**21, const int id = MEMORY_ID, const int RC = 2,
                       const int bytes = 8,

                       // Mutable RAM block -- mirrors `Mem(...)`
                       const int mem_base_address = 0, const int mem_size_mb = 128,
                       const int mem_large_mem = 0, const int mem_dual_mem = 0,
                       const int mem_lanes_x_row = 1,

                       // Free-input block -- mirrors `Mem(..., free_input_mem: 1)`
                       const int input_base_address = 0, const int input_size_mb = 128,
                       const int input_large_mem = 0, const int input_continuous_addresses = 0,
                       const int input_lanes_x_row = 1,

                       // Read-only program data block -- mirrors `RomData(...)`. It takes no
                       // base_address / size_mb: `RomData` declares them but never uses them, the
                       // address range is enforced by the INIT-comes-from-the-ROM mechanism.
                       const int rom_lanes_x_row = 1) {

    // The `enable_flag` of the three areas is deliberately absent: it gates only the global update
    // that OPENS a continuation cycle, and this air opens none of the three (see the header).

    assert(bytes == 8);

    // The three blocks share the first/last row selectors: they are a property of the air, not of
    // the block. `get_L1()` declares `__L1__` once per air, so the three blocks get the same one.
    const expr SEGMENT_L1 = get_L1();
    const expr SEGMENT_LAST = SEGMENT_L1';

    // Witness generation maps virtual rows to (row, lane) with a shift and a mask
    // (see MemLanes in state-machines/mem-common/src/mem_lanes.rs), so every lane count must be a
    // power of two.
    assert((mem_lanes_x_row & (mem_lanes_x_row - 1)) == 0,
           `mem_lanes_x_row must be a power of two but it's ${mem_lanes_x_row}`);
    assert((input_lanes_x_row & (input_lanes_x_row - 1)) == 0,
           `input_lanes_x_row must be a power of two but it's ${input_lanes_x_row}`);
    assert((rom_lanes_x_row & (rom_lanes_x_row - 1)) == 0,
           `rom_lanes_x_row must be a power of two but it's ${rom_lanes_x_row}`);

    // index of the last lane of a row, the lane used by the continuation values of the segment.
    const int MEM_LAST_LANE = mem_lanes_x_row - 1;
    const int INPUT_LAST_LANE = input_lanes_x_row - 1;
    const int ROM_LAST_LANE = rom_lanes_x_row - 1;

    // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // BLOCK 1 of 3 -- MUTABLE RAM
    //
    // Transcription of `Mem` (state-machines/mem/pil/mem.pil) specialized to a mutable, non
    // free-input memory (immutable = 0, free_input_mem = 0), with every name prefixed `mem_`.
    // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    assert(mem_size_mb <= 128 || mem_large_mem);
    assert(mem_lanes_x_row >= 1);

    const expr mem_internal_base_address = mem_base_address / bytes;
    const expr mem_internal_end_address = (mem_base_address + mem_size_mb * 1024 * 1024 - 1) / bytes;

    airval mem_segment_id;
    airval mem_is_first_segment;
    airval mem_is_last_segment;

    mem_is_first_segment * (1 - mem_is_first_segment) === 0;
    mem_is_last_segment * (1 - mem_is_last_segment) === 0;

    // If is_first_segment means that segment must be 0
    mem_is_first_segment * mem_segment_id === 0;

    col witness bits(29) mem_addr[mem_lanes_x_row];
    col witness bits(MEM_STEP_BITS) mem_step[mem_lanes_x_row];
    col witness bits(1) mem_sel[mem_lanes_x_row];
    col witness bits(1) mem_addr_changes[mem_lanes_x_row];
    col witness bits(32) mem_value[mem_lanes_x_row][RC];
    col witness bits(1) mem_wr[mem_lanes_x_row];

    const expr mem_rd[mem_lanes_x_row];

    if (mem_dual_mem) {
        // Two columns by lane, one for step (timestamp) of second operation, and other
        // to enable dual operation in the lane.
        col witness bits(MEM_STEP_BITS) air.mem_step_dual[mem_lanes_x_row];
        col witness bits(1) air.mem_sel_dual[mem_lanes_x_row];

        for (int lane = 0; lane < mem_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
            mem_sel_dual[lane] * (1 - mem_sel_dual[lane]) === 0;
            (1 - mem_sel[lane]) * mem_sel_dual[lane] === 0;
        }
    }

    for (int lane = 0; lane < mem_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        mem_sel[lane] * (1 - mem_sel[lane]) === 0;
        mem_addr_changes[lane] * (1 - mem_addr_changes[lane]) === 0;

        mem_rd[lane] = 1 - mem_wr[lane];

        // boolean constraint over wr;
        mem_wr[lane] * (1 - mem_wr[lane]) === 0;

        // all writes must be "launched" to the bus, not allowed silent writes
        mem_wr[lane] * (1 - mem_sel[lane]) === 0;
    }

    // CONTINUATIONS

    airval mem_previous_segment_value[RC];
    airval mem_previous_segment_step, mem_previous_segment_addr;

    // this constraint "assumes" that previous values of this segment
    direct_update_assumes(MEMORY_CONTINUATION_ID,
                  [
                    mem_base_address,               // identify area of memory
                    mem_segment_id,                 // current segment_id
                    mem_is_first_segment,           // to verify that first segment has the flag
                                                    // is_first_segment = 1 to match with direct global update
                    mem_previous_segment_addr,
                    mem_previous_segment_step,
                    ...mem_previous_segment_value
                  ]);

    airval mem_segment_last_value[RC];
    airval mem_segment_last_step, mem_segment_last_addr;

    for (int i = 0; i < RC; i++) {
        // The `segment_last_value[i]` air_value must match the `value[LAST_LANE][i]` from the last
        // row. In this case, it doesn't matter if it's dual or not, because the value is unique for
        // the last lane.
        SEGMENT_LAST * (mem_value[MEM_LAST_LANE][i] - mem_segment_last_value[i]) === 0;
    }

    // The `segment_last_addr` air_value must match the `addr[LAST_LANE]` from the last row.
    SEGMENT_LAST * (mem_addr[MEM_LAST_LANE] - mem_segment_last_addr) === 0;

    if (mem_dual_mem) {
        // The `segment_last_step` air_value must match the step from the last lane of the last row.
        // - If the last lane was in dual mode, it must match its `step_dual`.
        // - Otherwise, it must match its `step`.
        SEGMENT_LAST * (mem_sel_dual[MEM_LAST_LANE] * (mem_step_dual[MEM_LAST_LANE] - mem_step[MEM_LAST_LANE]) + mem_step[MEM_LAST_LANE] - mem_segment_last_step) === 0;
    } else {
        // The `segment_last_step` air_value must match the `step[LAST_LANE]` from the last row.
        SEGMENT_LAST * (mem_step[MEM_LAST_LANE] - mem_segment_last_step) === 0;
    }

    direct_update_proves(MEMORY_CONTINUATION_ID,
                  [
                    mem_base_address,               // identify area of memory
                    mem_segment_id + 1,             // next segment_id, for last segment
                    0,                              // is_first_segment must be 0 always on next segments
                    mem_segment_last_addr,          // last addr of segment
                    mem_segment_last_step,          // last step of segment
                    ...mem_segment_last_value
                  ],
                  sel: (1 - mem_is_last_segment));

    // The cycle of this memory area was already opened by the `Mem` air (see the file header): the
    // `proof.memory` container of mem.pil records which air did it, and this block only checks that
    // it is already recorded. It is an error to find it unopened -- this air proves a segment of a
    // cycle that nobody would open.
    compact_mem_check_area_seeded(mem_base_address);

    // For security, ensure that the first address has the correct value to prevent adding a large
    // number of instances that could cause an "overflow" in the prime field.

    if (mem_large_mem) {
        // Distance between the internal base address of memory area and previous_segment_address
        // could be greater than 2^24, so we divide this distance in two chunks of 16 bits, to verify
        // that this distance is "positive"

        airval air.mem_distance_base[2];

        mem_previous_segment_addr - mem_internal_base_address === mem_distance_base[0] + 0x10000 * mem_distance_base[1];

        range_check(expression: mem_distance_base[0], min: 0, max: 0xFFFF);
        range_check(expression: mem_distance_base[1], min: 0, max: 0xFFFF);
    } else {
        range_check(expression: mem_previous_segment_addr - mem_internal_base_address, min: 0, max: 2**24 - 1);
    }

    // Final memory control: the last address must be within the memory area.

    if (mem_large_mem) {
        airval air.mem_distance_end[2];

        mem_internal_end_address - mem_segment_last_addr === mem_distance_end[0] + 0x10000 * mem_distance_end[1];

        range_check(expression: mem_distance_end[0], min: 0, max: 0xFFFF);
        range_check(expression: mem_distance_end[1], min: 0, max: 0xFFFF);
        assert(mem_size_mb <= 4096);
    } else {
        range_check(expression: mem_internal_end_address - mem_segment_last_addr, min: 0, max: 2**24 - 1);
        assert(mem_size_mb <= 128);
    }

    // Check the order of operations inside segment
    //
    // `mem_previous_addr[lane]` / `mem_previous_step[lane]` are the values of the lane that precedes
    // lane `l`: lane `l - 1` of the same row when l > 0, and the last lane (MEM_LAST_LANE) of the
    // previous row when l == 0. On the first row (SEGMENT_L1) lane 0 takes the values of the
    // previous segment.

    const expr mem_previous_addr[mem_lanes_x_row];

    // The first lane must be "different" to force `address_change`. For this reason, we subtract
    // one from `previous_segment_addr` which in the first segment is equal to the first address
    // of the memory area.

    mem_previous_addr[0] = SEGMENT_L1 * (mem_previous_segment_addr - mem_is_first_segment - 'mem_addr[MEM_LAST_LANE]) + 'mem_addr[MEM_LAST_LANE];
    for (int lane = 1; lane < mem_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        mem_previous_addr[lane] = mem_addr[lane-1];
    }

    const expr mem_delta_addr[mem_lanes_x_row];
    for (int lane = 0; lane < mem_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        mem_delta_addr[lane] = mem_addr[lane] - mem_previous_addr[lane];
    }

    // on first lane of first row of first segment could be the same and address_change = 1 because
    // it's as a new addr
    // SEGMENT_L1 * (x + is_first_segment * SEGMENT_L1) === SEGMENT_L1 * (x + is_first_segment)

    if (mem_dual_mem) {
        // The `previous_step` column must match with the `step` from the previous lane.
        // - If the previous lane was in dual mode, it must match its `step_dual`.
        // - Otherwise, it must match its `step`.
        // An intermediate witness column is used to keep the degree of the increment constraint
        // at 2, because the "previous step" is a degree 2 expression.

        col witness bits(40) air.mem_previous_step[mem_lanes_x_row];

        const expr mem_previous_lane_step = 'mem_sel_dual[MEM_LAST_LANE] * ('mem_step_dual[MEM_LAST_LANE] - 'mem_step[MEM_LAST_LANE]) + 'mem_step[MEM_LAST_LANE];
        mem_previous_step[0] <== SEGMENT_L1 * (mem_previous_segment_step - mem_previous_lane_step) + mem_previous_lane_step;

        for (int lane = 1; lane < mem_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
            mem_previous_step[lane] <== mem_sel_dual[lane-1] * (mem_step_dual[lane-1] - mem_step[lane-1]) + mem_step[lane-1];
        }
    } else {
        const expr air.mem_previous_step[mem_lanes_x_row];

        mem_previous_step[0] = SEGMENT_L1 * (mem_previous_segment_step - 'mem_step[MEM_LAST_LANE]) + 'mem_step[MEM_LAST_LANE];
        for (int lane = 1; lane < mem_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
            mem_previous_step[lane] = mem_step[lane-1];
        }
    }

    col witness bits(22) mem_l_increment[mem_lanes_x_row];
    col witness bits(16) mem_h_increment[mem_lanes_x_row];

    // to avoid intermediate column
    col witness bits(1) mem_read_same_addr[mem_lanes_x_row];

    const expr mem_delta_step[mem_lanes_x_row];

    for (int lane = 0; lane < mem_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        mem_delta_step[lane] = mem_step[lane] - mem_previous_step[lane] + (1 - mem_wr[lane]);

        mem_l_increment[lane] + 2**22 * mem_h_increment[lane] + 1 === mem_addr_changes[lane] * (mem_delta_addr[lane] - mem_delta_step[lane]) + mem_delta_step[lane];

        // addr_change == 1 => 22 + 16 = 38 bits + 3 => 41 bits => 2 TB (limit address 32 bits)
        // addr_change == 0 => 22 + 16 = 38 bits => 4 slots => 2^36 => 32 GB steps (16K main instances)
        // 38 bits x 2^22 = 2^60 + 2^32 address = 2^61 secure.
        // using control in the middle of instance, reduce to 2^60

        range_check(expression: mem_l_increment[lane], min: 0, max: 2**22 - 1);
        range_check(expression: mem_h_increment[lane], min: 0, max: 2**16 - 1);

        mem_read_same_addr[lane] <== (1 - mem_addr_changes[lane]) * (1 - mem_wr[lane]);

        if (mem_dual_mem) {
            // Range check to validate that `step_dual` > `step` if the lane is dual. In case of
            // operations RR, `step_dual` could be equal to `step`, but in operation WR, `step_dual`
            // must be strictly greater than `step`. How dual operation must be in same chunk, with
            // range_check of 2^22 is enough if chunk_size <= 2^18.
            range_check(expression: mem_step_dual[lane] - mem_step[lane] - mem_wr[lane], min: 0, max: 2**22 - 1, sel: mem_sel_dual[lane]);
        }
    }

    mem_is_first_segment * SEGMENT_L1 * (1 - mem_addr_changes[0]) === 0;

    // if addr_changes is 0, check that previous address match with current addr.

    for (int lane = 0; lane < mem_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        (1 - mem_addr_changes[lane]) * (mem_addr[lane] - mem_previous_addr[lane]) === 0;
    }

    // PADDING: At end of memory fill with same addr, incrementing step, same value, sel = 0, rd = 1,
    //          wr = 0, setting is_last_segment = 1

    const expr mem_previous_value[mem_lanes_x_row][RC];
    for (int index = 0; index < RC; index++) {
        mem_previous_value[0][index] = SEGMENT_L1 * (mem_previous_segment_value[index] - 'mem_value[MEM_LAST_LANE][index]) + 'mem_value[MEM_LAST_LANE][index];
        for (int lane = 1; lane < mem_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
            mem_previous_value[lane][index] = mem_value[lane-1][index];
        }
    }

    for (int lane = 0; lane < mem_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        for (int index = 0; index < RC; index++) {
            // if address not change and it isn't write, value must be equal to previous value
            mem_read_same_addr[lane] * (mem_value[lane][index] - mem_previous_value[lane][index]) === 0;

            // if address changes, and it isn't a write, value must be 0.
            mem_addr_changes[lane] * (1 - mem_wr[lane]) * mem_value[lane][index] === 0;
        }
    }

    // The Memory component is only able to prove aligned memory access, since we force the bus
    // address to be a multiple of bytes and the width to be exactly bytes.

    const expr mem_op[mem_lanes_x_row];
    for (int lane = 0; lane < mem_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        mem_op[lane] = mem_wr[lane] * (MEMORY_STORE_OP - MEMORY_LOAD_OP) + MEMORY_LOAD_OP;
        permutation_proves(id, expressions: [mem_op[lane], mem_addr[lane] * bytes, mem_step[lane], bytes, ...mem_value[lane]], sel: mem_sel[lane], sel_is_binary: 1);

        if (mem_dual_mem) {
            // dual mem memory, send to bus always a READ operation, with same address, and value,
            // only change de step.
            permutation_proves(id, expressions: [MEMORY_LOAD_OP, mem_addr[lane] * bytes, mem_step_dual[lane], bytes, ...mem_value[lane]], sel: mem_sel_dual[lane], sel_is_binary: 1);
        }
    }

    // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // BLOCK 2 of 3 -- FREE-INPUT MEMORY
    //
    // Transcription of `Mem` (state-machines/mem/pil/mem.pil) specialized to free_input_mem = 1
    // (which forces immutable = 1 and forbids dual_mem), with every name prefixed `input_`.
    // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    assert(input_size_mb <= 128 || input_large_mem);
    assert(input_lanes_x_row >= 1);

    const expr input_internal_base_address = input_base_address / bytes;
    const expr input_internal_end_address = (input_base_address + input_size_mb * 1024 * 1024 - 1) / bytes;

    airval input_segment_id;
    airval input_is_first_segment;
    airval input_is_last_segment;

    input_is_first_segment * (1 - input_is_first_segment) === 0;
    input_is_last_segment * (1 - input_is_last_segment) === 0;

    input_is_first_segment * input_segment_id === 0;

    col witness bits(29) input_addr[input_lanes_x_row];
    col witness bits(MEM_STEP_BITS) input_step[input_lanes_x_row];
    col witness bits(1) input_sel[input_lanes_x_row];
    col witness bits(1) input_addr_changes[input_lanes_x_row];
    col witness bits(16) input_value_word[input_lanes_x_row][RC*2];

    // free read for free input, means an specific address (offset = 0) that could be read
    // many times with different well formed 64-bit values. Used for fcalls and other free-inputs
    col witness bits(1) input_is_free_read[input_lanes_x_row];

    const expr input_value[input_lanes_x_row][RC];

    // free input memory must be read-only
    const expr input_wr[input_lanes_x_row];

    for (int lane = 0; lane < input_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        input_sel[lane] * (1 - input_sel[lane]) === 0;
        input_addr_changes[lane] * (1 - input_addr_changes[lane]) === 0;

        input_wr[lane] = 0;

        input_is_free_read[lane] * (1 - input_is_free_read[lane]) === 0;
        input_is_free_read[lane] * (input_internal_base_address - input_addr[lane]) === 0;

        for (int index = 0; index < RC; ++index) {
            input_value[lane][index] = input_value_word[lane][index*2] + MAX_RANGE_CHECK_CHUNK * input_value_word[lane][index*2 + 1];

            // how value is a free-input, must be checked that it's 32-bit well formed value
            range_check(input_value_word[lane][index*2], 0, MAX_RANGE_CHECK_CHUNK - 1);
            range_check(input_value_word[lane][index*2+1], 0, MAX_RANGE_CHECK_CHUNK - 1);
        }
    }

    // CONTINUATIONS

    airval input_previous_segment_value[RC];
    airval input_previous_segment_step, input_previous_segment_addr;

    direct_update_assumes(MEMORY_CONTINUATION_ID,
                  [
                    input_base_address,
                    input_segment_id,
                    input_is_first_segment,
                    input_previous_segment_addr,
                    input_previous_segment_step,
                    ...input_previous_segment_value
                  ]);

    airval input_segment_last_value[RC];
    airval input_segment_last_step, input_segment_last_addr;

    for (int i = 0; i < RC; i++) {
        SEGMENT_LAST * (input_value[INPUT_LAST_LANE][i] - input_segment_last_value[i]) === 0;
    }

    SEGMENT_LAST * (input_addr[INPUT_LAST_LANE] - input_segment_last_addr) === 0;
    SEGMENT_LAST * (input_step[INPUT_LAST_LANE] - input_segment_last_step) === 0;

    direct_update_proves(MEMORY_CONTINUATION_ID,
                  [
                    input_base_address,
                    input_segment_id + 1,
                    0,
                    input_segment_last_addr,
                    input_segment_last_step,
                    ...input_segment_last_value
                  ],
                  sel: (1 - input_is_last_segment));

    // Opened by the `InputData` air, see the header of this file and the `mem_` block above.
    compact_mem_check_area_seeded(input_base_address);

    if (input_large_mem) {
        airval air.input_distance_base[2];

        input_previous_segment_addr - input_internal_base_address === input_distance_base[0] + 0x10000 * input_distance_base[1];

        range_check(expression: input_distance_base[0], min: 0, max: 0xFFFF);
        range_check(expression: input_distance_base[1], min: 0, max: 0xFFFF);
    } else {
        range_check(expression: input_previous_segment_addr - input_internal_base_address, min: 0, max: 2**24 - 1);
    }

    if (input_large_mem) {
        airval air.input_distance_end[2];

        input_internal_end_address - input_segment_last_addr === input_distance_end[0] + 0x10000 * input_distance_end[1];

        range_check(expression: input_distance_end[0], min: 0, max: 0xFFFF);
        range_check(expression: input_distance_end[1], min: 0, max: 0xFFFF);
        assert(input_size_mb <= 4096);
    } else {
        range_check(expression: input_internal_end_address - input_segment_last_addr, min: 0, max: 2**24 - 1);
        assert(input_size_mb <= 128);
    }

    // Immutable means that all operations must produces the same value. In the case of free-input
    // memory, no write operations exist. The first read value for a given address determines all
    // subsequent values for that same address.
    //
    // In this type of memory, addresses need to be ordered. However, execution typically accesses a
    // full range of addresses. To avoid wasting a range check, we assume that addresses are
    // consecutive (note that addresses are `bytes` addresses). If the executor detects that an
    // address has not been accessed, it must be added but not sent to the bus.
    //
    // In the case of free-input memory, in first segment, if the first lane is equal to previous
    // addr, means that is_free_read is active, value is free. In case of the first lane is
    // different, the `address_change` is active, and value could change. With this consideration,
    // the first value of first lane is always free, and we could use 0 as previous value on global
    // constraint of first segment.

    const expr input_previous_addr[input_lanes_x_row];

    input_previous_addr[0] = SEGMENT_L1 * (input_previous_segment_addr - 'input_addr[INPUT_LAST_LANE]) + 'input_addr[INPUT_LAST_LANE];
    for (int lane = 1; lane < input_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        input_previous_addr[lane] = input_addr[lane-1];
    }

    // read_same_addr = (1 - addr_changes) * (1 - wr), and wr = 0 on free-input memory.
    const expr input_read_same_addr[input_lanes_x_row];
    const expr input_delta_addr[input_lanes_x_row];

    for (int lane = 0; lane < input_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        input_delta_addr[lane] = input_addr[lane] - input_previous_addr[lane];
        input_read_same_addr[lane] = (1 - input_addr_changes[lane]);

        if (input_continuous_addresses) {
            // If the address changes, the difference must be one
            input_addr_changes[lane] * (input_delta_addr[lane] - 1) === 0;
        } else {
            range_check(expression: input_delta_addr[lane] - 1, min: 0, max: 2**24 - 1, sel: input_addr_changes[lane]);
        }

        // If the address does not change, the current address and previous must be the same.
        // `mem.pil` states this one twice on the immutable path (here and again in the loop that
        // follows its if/else); once is enough. See the header of this file.
        (1 - input_addr_changes[lane]) * (input_addr[lane] - input_previous_addr[lane]) === 0;
    }

    const expr input_previous_value[input_lanes_x_row][RC];
    for (int index = 0; index < RC; index++) {
        input_previous_value[0][index] = SEGMENT_L1 * (input_previous_segment_value[index] - 'input_value[INPUT_LAST_LANE][index]) + 'input_value[INPUT_LAST_LANE][index];
        for (int lane = 1; lane < input_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
            input_previous_value[lane][index] = input_value[lane-1][index];
        }
    }

    for (int lane = 0; lane < input_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        for (int index = 0; index < RC; index++) {
            // if address not change value must be equal to previous value, when is_free_read
            // is active the value not change, so value must be equal to previous value
            (1 - input_addr_changes[lane]) * (1 - input_is_free_read[lane]) * (input_value[lane][index] - input_previous_value[lane][index]) === 0;
        }
    }

    const expr input_op[input_lanes_x_row];
    for (int lane = 0; lane < input_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        input_op[lane] = input_wr[lane] * (MEMORY_STORE_OP - MEMORY_LOAD_OP) + MEMORY_LOAD_OP;
        permutation_proves(id, expressions: [input_op[lane], input_addr[lane] * bytes, input_step[lane], bytes, ...input_value[lane]], sel: input_sel[lane], sel_is_binary: 1);
    }

    // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // BLOCK 3 of 3 -- READ-ONLY PROGRAM DATA
    //
    // Transcription of `RomData` (state-machines/mem/pil/rom_data.pil), with every name prefixed
    // `rom_`.
    // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    assert(rom_lanes_x_row >= 1);

    col witness bits(1) rom_addr_change[rom_lanes_x_row];
    col witness bits(29) rom_addr[rom_lanes_x_row];
    col witness bits(40) rom_step[rom_lanes_x_row];
    col witness bits(32) rom_value[rom_lanes_x_row][RC];

    // CONTINUATIONS

    airval rom_previous_segment_value[RC];
    airval rom_previous_segment_addr;

    airval rom_segment_last_value[RC];
    airval rom_segment_last_addr;

    airval rom_segment_id;
    airval rom_is_first_segment;
    airval rom_is_last_segment;

    rom_is_first_segment * (1 - rom_is_first_segment) === 0;
    rom_is_last_segment * (1 - rom_is_last_segment) === 0;

    rom_is_first_segment * rom_segment_id === 0;

    // Force in first segment the first lane of the first row must be addr_change to force a INIT
    // operation
    rom_is_first_segment * SEGMENT_L1 * (rom_addr_change[0] - 1) === 0;

    // ADDRESS GROUPING CONSTRAINTS
    //
    // addr_change = 1 forces step = MEMORY_INIT_STEP, linking the lane to an INIT operation.
    // When addr_change = 0, addr and value must equal the previous lane's values, ensuring
    // all accesses to the same address remain consecutive.

    for (int lane = 0; lane < rom_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        rom_addr_change[lane] * (1 - rom_addr_change[lane]) === 0;
        rom_addr_change[lane] * (rom_step[lane] - MEMORY_INIT_STEP) === 0;
    }

    const expr rom_previous_value[rom_lanes_x_row][RC];
    const expr rom_previous_addr[rom_lanes_x_row];

    for (int i = 0; i < RC; i++) {
        rom_previous_value[0][i] = SEGMENT_L1 * (rom_previous_segment_value[i] - 'rom_value[ROM_LAST_LANE][i]) + 'rom_value[ROM_LAST_LANE][i];
        for (int lane = 1; lane < rom_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
            rom_previous_value[lane][i] = rom_value[lane-1][i];
        }
        SEGMENT_LAST * (rom_segment_last_value[i] - rom_value[ROM_LAST_LANE][i]) === 0;
    }

    rom_previous_addr[0] = SEGMENT_L1 * (rom_previous_segment_addr - 'rom_addr[ROM_LAST_LANE]) + 'rom_addr[ROM_LAST_LANE];
    for (int lane = 1; lane < rom_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        rom_previous_addr[lane] = rom_addr[lane-1];
    }

    SEGMENT_LAST * (rom_segment_last_addr - rom_addr[ROM_LAST_LANE]) === 0;

    // BUS INTERACTION
    //
    // Every lane is sent to the memory bus as either a MEMORY_ROM_INIT_OP (when addr_change = 1,
    // i.e. the address is new) or a MEMORY_LOAD_OP (when addr_change = 0, i.e. the address
    // continues from the previous lane). Since the ROM is the sole source of INIT claims, only
    // addresses explicitly present in the ROM data can be proven valid.

    for (int lane = 0; lane < rom_lanes_x_row; ++lane) {
        for (int i = 0; i < RC; i++) {
            (1 - rom_addr_change[lane]) * (rom_value[lane][i] - rom_previous_value[lane][i]) === 0;
        }
        (1 - rom_addr_change[lane]) * (rom_addr[lane] - rom_previous_addr[lane]) === 0;

        permutation_proves(id, expressions: [rom_addr_change[lane] * (MEMORY_ROM_INIT_OP - MEMORY_LOAD_OP) + MEMORY_LOAD_OP, rom_addr[lane] * bytes, rom_step[lane], 8, ...rom_value[lane]]);
    }

    direct_update_assumes(ROM_DATA_CONTINUATION_ID, [ rom_segment_id, rom_is_first_segment, rom_previous_segment_addr, ...rom_previous_segment_value]);

    direct_update_proves(ROM_DATA_CONTINUATION_ID, [ rom_segment_id+1, 0, rom_segment_last_addr, ...rom_segment_last_value], sel: (1 - rom_is_last_segment));

    // Opened by the `RomData` air, see the header of this file.
    compact_mem_check_rom_data_seeded();

    // PADDING
    //
    // The padding, number of assumes, is limited only to the last segment (is_last_segment = 1) and
    // must be a positive number less than 2**24. It counts lanes, not rows.
    //
    // Security limitations:
    // • The memory operation is fixed (MEMORY_LOAD_OP): does not allow to start a new cycle
    // • The step value is fixed to MEMORY_INIT_STEP
    // • The addr and value must match the last values of the last lane (segment_last_addr and
    //   segment_last_value), assuming that the lanes are correct, these values are correct.

    airval rom_padding_size;
    lookup(id, expressions: [MEMORY_LOAD_OP, rom_segment_last_addr * 8, MEMORY_INIT_STEP, bytes, ...rom_segment_last_value], mul: -rom_padding_size);

    (1 - rom_is_last_segment) * rom_padding_size === 0;

    assert(N * rom_lanes_x_row <= 2**24);
    range_check(expression: rom_padding_size, min: 0, max: 2**24 - 1, sel: rom_is_last_segment);
}

// Checks that the continuation cycle of `base_address` has already been opened by another air --
// the `Mem`/`InputData` air of that area. `CompactMem` proves segment 0 of the cycle but must not
// open it: the global update that does is emitted once per air, statically, and a second one would
// leave the global sum unable to balance (see the comment on `proof.memory` in mem.pil).
private function compact_mem_check_area_seeded(const int base_address) {
    container proof.memory {
        int base_addresses_count = 0;
        int base_addresses[MAX_BASE_ADDRESSES];
        string base_addresses_labels[MAX_BASE_ADDRESSES];
    }
    use proof.memory;
    for (int index = 0; index < base_addresses_count; ++index) {
        if (base_addresses[index] == base_address) {
            println(`${AIR_NAME}: memory cycle of ${base_address} opened by ${base_addresses_labels[index]}`);
            return;
        }
    }
    error(`${AIR_NAME} proves a segment of the memory area ${base_address}, but no air opened its continuation cycle. Instantiate CompactMem after the Mem air of that area in pil/zisk.pil.`);
}

// Same as `compact_mem_check_area_seeded`, for the single cycle of `ROM_DATA_CONTINUATION_ID`,
// which is keyed by nothing (there is only one rom-data area).
private function compact_mem_check_rom_data_seeded() {
    container proof.rom_data {
        int seeded = 0;
        string seeded_by = "";
    }
    use proof.rom_data;
    if (!seeded) {
        error(`${AIR_NAME} proves a segment of the rom-data cycle, but no air opened it. Instantiate CompactMem after the RomData air in pil/zisk.pil.`);
    }
    println(`${AIR_NAME}: rom-data cycle opened by ${seeded_by}`);
}
